1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,597
Dinner is almost ready.

2
00:00:01,637 --> 00:00:05,080
You're stirring a pot and answering
a homework question at once.

3
00:00:05,526 --> 00:00:09,120
Suddenly there's a cup on the floor,
a little brother getting hit,

4
00:00:09,160 --> 00:00:11,279
and a voice getting louder and louder.

5
00:00:11,355 --> 00:00:14,531
And you think: why does
my child keep doing that?

6
00:00:14,722 --> 00:00:17,944
Here's the good news.
There's usually a reason.

7
00:00:18,077 --> 00:00:21,438
And once you find it,
you can often change the moment.

8
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:27,081
Coming up: the four reasons experts look
for first, a simple way to spot patterns,

9
00:00:27,121 --> 00:00:30,123
and one common habit that
can make things worse.

10
00:00:30,314 --> 00:00:35,104
Quick note: this is general information,
not advice about your child.

11
00:00:35,365 --> 00:00:38,564
For advice about your child,
or any health questions,

12
00:00:38,697 --> 00:00:42,882
talk with your child's doctor or
another qualified professional.

13
00:00:43,232 --> 00:00:48,219
Hitting. Screaming. Tantrums.
Running off. Here's the big idea.

14
00:00:48,352 --> 00:00:52,248
Behavior isn't random.
It usually does a job for the child.

15
00:00:52,439 --> 00:00:56,241
And if a behavior keeps happening,
that's often a sign it's working.

16
00:00:56,595 --> 00:00:59,341
Not because your child is
being sneaky or difficult.

17
00:00:59,381 --> 00:01:02,302
It simply worked before,
so it happens again.

18
00:01:02,562 --> 00:01:04,891
And a purpose doesn't mean on purpose.

19
00:01:05,082 --> 00:01:07,526
Your child usually
isn't planning anything.

20
00:01:07,601 --> 00:01:09,209
Grown-ups do this too.

21
00:01:09,401 --> 00:01:13,331
If jiggling a sticky door handle opens it,
you'll jiggle it every time.

22
00:01:13,522 --> 00:01:15,142
Kids learn the same way.

23
00:01:15,217 --> 00:01:18,892
When yelling gets a parent to look up,
or a tantrum ends bath time,

24
00:01:18,932 --> 00:01:21,063
your child learns: that worked.

25
00:01:21,417 --> 00:01:24,686
So instead of asking,
"How do I stop this?", ask,

26
00:01:24,726 --> 00:01:26,914
"What is this doing for my child?"

27
00:01:28,616 --> 00:01:31,897
That one question can
change what you try next.

28
00:01:32,247 --> 00:01:35,504
Behavior experts sort
these jobs into four kinds.

29
00:01:35,858 --> 00:01:39,068
One: attention. Picture this.

30
00:01:39,259 --> 00:01:41,042
You sit down to make a phone call,

31
00:01:41,082 --> 00:01:46,661
and soon your child is climbing the couch
or poking their sister. Even "Stop that!"

32
00:01:46,701 --> 00:01:51,537
is attention. For some kids,
any attention beats no attention at all.

33
00:01:51,612 --> 00:01:54,695
Two: escape. Picture this.

34
00:01:54,828 --> 00:02:00,210
You say, "Time for homework," or "Bath
time," and the tears and yelling start.

35
00:02:00,656 --> 00:02:05,678
If the homework or the bath gets skipped,
that big reaction may have done a job:

36
00:02:05,753 --> 00:02:08,766
it got your child out of
something hard, for now.

37
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:15,120
Sometimes it's also a message: this is
too hard, too long, or too much right now.

38
00:02:15,204 --> 00:02:16,974
That's worth listening to.

39
00:02:17,108 --> 00:02:18,890
Some meltdowns are different.

40
00:02:19,024 --> 00:02:22,826
They come from overwhelm,
like too much noise or change.

41
00:02:23,087 --> 00:02:26,483
An overloaded child needs
comfort and calm first.

42
00:02:26,744 --> 00:02:31,174
Those moments still have a cause,
and ABC notes can help you see it.

43
00:02:31,365 --> 00:02:34,901
Three: getting things. Picture this.

44
00:02:35,092 --> 00:02:39,673
You're in the checkout line, and
there's candy at eye level. You say no.

45
00:02:39,864 --> 00:02:41,344
The screaming starts.

46
00:02:41,420 --> 00:02:45,977
If the candy lands in the cart, your
child may learn that screaming gets candy.

47
00:02:46,423 --> 00:02:48,415
Four: automatic.

48
00:02:48,606 --> 00:02:52,873
Some behaviors feel good,
or calm the body, all by themselves.

49
00:02:53,134 --> 00:02:56,647
Think humming, rocking,
or chewing on a shirt collar.

50
00:02:56,780 --> 00:03:02,150
They'd happen even if no one were around.
Many are harmless and fine to leave alone.

51
00:03:02,411 --> 00:03:06,945
Think about a hard moment from this week.
Which job might it have been doing?

52
00:03:07,206 --> 00:03:11,090
One behavior can even do
different jobs at different times.

53
00:03:11,439 --> 00:03:17,215
To spot patterns, try a simple ABC note.
The A is what happened right before.

54
00:03:17,290 --> 00:03:21,627
The B is what your child did.
The C is what happened right after.

55
00:03:21,818 --> 00:03:26,573
Here's an example. Before:
I asked him to put his shoes on.

56
00:03:26,648 --> 00:03:30,300
What he did: he threw a
shoe and ran to his room.

57
00:03:30,491 --> 00:03:33,319
Right after: I put his shoes on for him.

58
00:03:33,394 --> 00:03:36,070
Write what you saw,
not what you think it meant.

59
00:03:36,203 --> 00:03:39,634
Try it for about a week,
for the hardest behavior right now.

60
00:03:39,826 --> 00:03:43,639
Does it happen at the same time
of day? With the same task?

61
00:03:44,086 --> 00:03:49,212
Does the "after" part keep looking
the same? Notice the easy times, too.

62
00:03:49,403 --> 00:03:52,858
When does it not happen?
And notice the bigger picture.

63
00:03:53,049 --> 00:03:58,825
Is your child hungry, tired, or unwell?
Did something change at home or school?

64
00:03:59,086 --> 00:04:04,943
Those can make hard moments more likely.
An ABC note is a clue, not a final answer.

65
00:04:05,297 --> 00:04:10,249
There's a free printable ABC sheet for
your fridge, linked in the description.

66
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:14,321
Once you have a good guess about
the job, pick a plan that fits.

67
00:04:14,513 --> 00:04:18,385
Which one sounds most like
your house? Let's start there.

68
00:04:18,576 --> 00:04:21,183
If it's attention, plan attention ahead.

69
00:04:21,537 --> 00:04:25,176
Before a call, give your child
a few minutes of full focus

70
00:04:25,216 --> 00:04:29,251
and a special activity for while
you talk. Then check in often.

71
00:04:29,443 --> 00:04:33,501
And catch them being good:
"You're playing so nicely. Thank you!"

72
00:04:33,692 --> 00:04:37,135
Save your biggest attention for the
behavior you want to see more of.

73
00:04:37,326 --> 00:04:40,270
If it's escape,
make the hard thing doable.

74
00:04:40,345 --> 00:04:45,854
Break homework into small chunks, starting
with an easy problem. Offer short breaks.

75
00:04:45,929 --> 00:04:48,095
Check that the task fits your child.

76
00:04:48,286 --> 00:04:52,797
And teach a better way to ask,
like "Break, please," or a break card.

77
00:04:53,151 --> 00:04:56,431
At first, say yes every
time they ask that way.

78
00:04:56,622 --> 00:04:59,194
Saying yes teaches them
that their words work.

79
00:04:59,455 --> 00:05:02,410
Then, slowly, the work time can stretch.

80
00:05:02,764 --> 00:05:07,089
If it's getting things, teach a
way to ask, like "Can I have one?"

81
00:05:07,443 --> 00:05:11,930
Use first-then: "First we pay,
then you can pick a sticker."

82
00:05:12,493 --> 00:05:15,518
Praise calm waiting.
And keep your promise.

83
00:05:15,709 --> 00:05:19,279
When the answer has to be no,
say it calmly, once.

84
00:05:19,471 --> 00:05:21,392
Then offer something they can have.

85
00:05:21,746 --> 00:05:24,794
If it's automatic,
leave the harmless ones alone.

86
00:05:24,985 --> 00:05:29,124
If one is unsafe or gets in the way,
like chewing things that could break,

87
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:32,770
ask your child's doctor or
therapist about safer choices.

88
00:05:33,217 --> 00:05:35,359
One more thing, for any reason.

89
00:05:35,492 --> 00:05:39,910
If your child hurts themselves, like
head-banging or biting their own hand,

90
00:05:39,950 --> 00:05:43,207
or hurts other people,
don't try to handle it alone.

91
00:05:43,654 --> 00:05:47,491
Talk with their doctor, since pain or
illness can be part of the picture,

92
00:05:47,531 --> 00:05:49,755
and ask about a behavior specialist.

93
00:05:50,202 --> 00:05:55,897
If anyone is in danger right now,
call 911 or your local emergency number.

94
00:05:56,247 --> 00:06:00,769
Now, what usually doesn't help?
First: only punishing.

95
00:06:01,123 --> 00:06:03,626
Punishment might stop a behavior for now,

96
00:06:03,666 --> 00:06:08,351
but it doesn't teach your child what to
do instead, and the need is still there.

97
00:06:08,797 --> 00:06:13,192
If the behavior was for attention,
a big reaction might even feed it.

98
00:06:13,453 --> 00:06:16,385
Harsh punishment can also
strain your relationship.

99
00:06:16,832 --> 00:06:20,809
Second: giving in after
things get big. Be honest.

100
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,977
Have you ever given in just to
make it stop? Most of us have.

101
00:06:25,610 --> 00:06:30,272
Picture saying no to candy three times,
then yes once the screaming gets loud.

102
00:06:30,463 --> 00:06:33,941
That can teach that louder works.
Here's the difference.

103
00:06:33,981 --> 00:06:36,657
Saying yes to a calm ask is teaching.

104
00:06:36,849 --> 00:06:41,499
Saying yes after the screaming is giving
in. So say yes early when you can.

105
00:06:41,690 --> 00:06:44,633
Once you've said no, stay calm and steady.

106
00:06:44,894 --> 00:06:46,584
When you change your response,

107
00:06:46,659 --> 00:06:51,460
behavior can get bigger for a little
while before it settles. That's common.

108
00:06:51,721 --> 00:06:55,674
Stay close and kind, and remind
them of the calm way to ask.

109
00:06:55,866 --> 00:07:01,595
Holding steady never means ignoring a
child who is hurt, scared or truly upset.

110
00:07:02,042 --> 00:07:06,936
If things get unsafe, safety comes first,
and it's time to bring in a professional.

111
00:07:07,197 --> 00:07:10,338
If you've done both of these,
you're not a bad parent.

112
00:07:10,529 --> 00:07:16,224
You're a tired human who wanted the moment
to end. What matters is what you try next.

113
00:07:16,573 --> 00:07:18,762
So when is it time to get more help?

114
00:07:19,116 --> 00:07:23,917
Get help if a behavior is dangerous,
or your child hurts themselves or others.

115
00:07:24,270 --> 00:07:28,328
Get help if it gets in the way
of school, sleep or family life,

116
00:07:28,404 --> 00:07:30,570
or if behavior changes suddenly.

117
00:07:30,831 --> 00:07:35,237
A sudden change can mean pain or illness,
so check with the doctor, too.

118
00:07:35,370 --> 00:07:37,385
And get help if you feel stuck.

119
00:07:37,576 --> 00:07:42,667
A Board Certified Behavior
Analyst, or BCBA, can help.

120
00:07:43,021 --> 00:07:47,671
They often start with a functional
behavior assessment, or FBA.

121
00:07:48,025 --> 00:07:51,259
In plain words,
that's careful detective work.

122
00:07:51,450 --> 00:07:55,102
They talk with you,
watch your child in everyday places,

123
00:07:55,142 --> 00:07:57,911
and look at notes like your ABC sheet.

124
00:07:58,358 --> 00:08:01,882
They may also work with your
child's doctor or teachers.

125
00:08:02,329 --> 00:08:04,611
Then they build a plan with your family,

126
00:08:04,651 --> 00:08:07,803
one that fits the reason
and teaches new skills.

127
00:08:08,064 --> 00:08:11,182
You should understand and
agree with every part of it.

128
00:08:11,315 --> 00:08:14,920
A good plan also respects your
child's comfort and choices,

129
00:08:14,960 --> 00:08:17,520
and changes if your child is unhappy.

130
00:08:17,711 --> 00:08:21,409
A large body of research
supports quality ABA,

131
00:08:21,484 --> 00:08:27,017
and the ideas in this video come from that
research. Still, every child is different.

132
00:08:27,208 --> 00:08:29,989
No honest provider will
promise an outcome.

133
00:08:30,180 --> 00:08:34,842
A good one will treat you as a partner,
because you know your child best.

134
00:08:35,192 --> 00:08:38,066
So remember: behavior has a purpose.

135
00:08:38,512 --> 00:08:42,129
Find the job, and you can find
a better way to meet the need.

136
00:08:42,576 --> 00:08:46,924
This week, grab the free ABC
sheet and try it for one behavior.

137
00:08:47,371 --> 00:08:53,089
Next time: first-then, step by step.
Subscribe so you don't miss it.

138
00:08:53,222 --> 00:08:57,025
And tell us in the comments:
which reason do you see most at home?

139
00:08:57,286 --> 00:09:00,960
Just type the number:
one, two, three or four.

140
00:09:01,221 --> 00:09:03,945
Thanks for watching,
and be kind to yourself today.
